  "content": {
    "id": "p31-q90-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Let $f(x)=\\sin^4 x+\\cos^4 x$ for $0\\le x\\le 2\\pi$.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p31-q90-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Solve $\\sin^4 x+\\cos^4 x=1$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p31-q90-a-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "x=0,\\ \\frac{\\pi}{2},\\ \\pi,\\ \\frac{3\\pi}{2},\\ 2\\pi",
                    "display": false
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p31-q90-a-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Use the Pythagorean identity to obtain ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "\\sin^2 x\\cos^2 x=0",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "Using $\\sin^2 x+\\cos^2 x=1$,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\sin^4 x+\\cos^4 x&=(\\sin^2 x+\\cos^2 x)^2-2\\sin^2 x\\cos^2 x\\\\\\sin^4 x+\\cos^4 x&=1-2\\sin^2 x\\cos^2 x.\\end{align*}$$\nHence,\n$$\\begin{align*}1-2\\sin^2 x\\cos^2 x&=1\\\\\\sin^2 x\\cos^2 x&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $\\sin x=0$ or $\\cos x=0$. On $0\\le x\\le2\\pi$,\n$$x=0,\\ \\frac{\\pi}{2},\\ \\pi,\\ \\frac{3\\pi}{2},\\ 2\\pi.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 68
      },
      {
        "id": "p31-q90-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Solve $\\sin^4 x+\\cos^4 x=\\frac12$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p31-q90-b-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "x=\\frac{\\pi}{4},\\ \\frac{3\\pi}{4},\\ \\frac{5\\pi}{4},\\ \\frac{7\\pi}{4}",
                    "display": false
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p31-q90-b-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Substitute ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "\\cos^2 x=1-\\sin^2 x",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " and factor the resulting quadratic.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\sin^4 x+(1-\\sin^2 x)^2&=\\frac12\\\\2\\sin^4 x-2\\sin^2 x+1&=\\frac12\\\\4\\sin^4 x-4\\sin^2 x+1&=0\\\\(2\\sin^2 x-1)^2&=0\\\\\\sin^2 x&=\\frac12\\\\\\sin x&=\\pm\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}.\\end{align*}$$\nThe related angle is $\\frac{\\pi}{4}$, with both signs included. On $0\\le x\\le2\\pi$,\n$$x=\\frac{\\pi}{4},\\ \\frac{3\\pi}{4},\\ \\frac{5\\pi}{4},\\ \\frac{7\\pi}{4}.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 74
      },
      {
        "id": "p31-q90-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "For what value(s) of $k$ does $f(x)=k$ have solutions?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p31-q90-c-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "\\frac12\\le k\\le1",
                    "display": false
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p31-q90-c-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Write ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "f(x)=2\\left(\\sin^2 x-\\frac12\\right)^2+\\frac12",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " and use ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "0\\le\\sin^2 x\\le1",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f(x)&=\\sin^4 x+(1-\\sin^2 x)^2\\\\f(x)&=2\\sin^4 x-2\\sin^2 x+1\\\\f(x)&=2\\left(\\sin^2 x-\\frac12\\right)^2+\\frac12.\\end{align*}$$\nSince $0\\le\\sin^2 x\\le1$,\n$$0\\le\\left(\\sin^2 x-\\frac12\\right)^2\\le\\frac14.$$\nTherefore $\\frac12\\le f(x)\\le1$. The minimum occurs when $\\sin^2 x=\\frac12$ and the maximum when $\\sin^2 x=0$ or $1$. As $\\sin^2 x$ takes every value in $[0,1]$ on the stated domain, every value in this range is attained.\n$$\\frac12\\le k\\le1.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 82
      }
    ]
  },
